  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia provided extensive technical support, resources and practical assistance to Vanuatu&#x2019;s Ministry of Health to strengthen its services at the provincial level by identifying root causes of health service weakness and targeting reform at the site. The partnership is working to broker relationships between Ministry of Health and the Sanma provincial government to pilot a primary health care model through the Tasmalum Health Centre Pilot. This pilot will drive reform from the provincial level and address bottlenecks to health service delivery.  Lessons from the pilot will influence decentralisation of health services more broadly across the six provinces.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Following the outbreak of COVID-19 in Vanuatu in 2022, Australia helped Vanuatu avoid the severe impacts experienced elsewhere by supporting the Ministry of Health (MOH) with outbreak preparedness and response. Australia worked intensively with the MOH Surveillance Unit to ensure timely analysis and recommendations for policymakers.  Australia also supported provincial governments to accelerate funding for vaccination coverage, particularly in hard to reach areas. Australia also supported MOH to address emerging health threats and community priorities including a catch-up measles vaccination campaign and malaria surge teams. As a result, a measles outbreak was avoided and malaria outbreaks have been controlled.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In 2022 the program supported the Ministry of Health and other government departments to collaborate on and progress actions towards reforming and improving primary health care service delivery, a key aspect of the 10-year Health Sector Strategy. The program supported Government of Vanuatu partners to convene dialogue at the national level and coordinate collaboration on priorities.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Malaria is a key public health risk for Vanuatu.  In 2024, Vanuatu experienced a malaria outbreak. The outbreak was particularly severe in Torba Province, where the annual parasitic incidence (API) tripled to 65 per 1,000 population in 2023, compared to the elimination target of an API of 1. Australia worked alongside the Ministry of Health to support the outbreak response. Australia funded a full-time World Health Organisation technical advisor to support the Ministry to develop a response strategy and malaria case management guidelines. Australia also provided direct funding to the Ministry to support outbreak control interventions, supervisory visits from malaria officers from Ministry of Health, disease prevention implementation activities, and community awareness campaigns. As a result of the interventions, the API for Torba in 2024 was reduced by more than a third to an API of 41. In addition to outbreak response, Australia provides ongoing support to progress Vanuatu&#x2019;s goal of malaria elimination by 2030.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Vanuatu Australia Health Partnership (VAHP) supports the Ministry of Health to strengthen Vanuatu&#x2019;s health system to deliver equitable, inclusive health services. It also delivers a systems-wide approach to health facility reconstruction following natural disasters. Demonstrating this approach, in Sanma Province, Australia has ensured the Tasmalum Health Centre is a model for best practice decentralised primary healthcare. Australia funded the reconstruction of the Health Centre after it was damaged during Tropical Cyclone Harold in 2020. A local construction company completed the project, contracted and managed through Vanuatu Government systems, with oversight and support from VAHP. Australia concurrently strengthened Tasmalum Health Centre&#x2019;s service delivery by enhancing provincial governance and referral policies and improving health administration and data collection. Since the facility&#x2019;s reopening, patient numbers rose from 551 patients in the October to December quarter in 2023, to 840 patients in the October to December quarter in 2024.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
